<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Metz</h1><p class="st-meta">1189 CE – 1552 CE (approx.) · capital Metz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing patrician republic in Lorraine, one of the richest cities of the Empire, which France seized and held against Charles V in 1552.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> gradually took power from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> during the 12th century, and by the late 12th century the city governed itself under the emperor's protection, outside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-metz">Prince-Bishopric of Metz</a>. A master-échevin elected each year headed the city government from 1180.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> was run by the paraiges, a handful of patrician family associations that monopolised offices. Its merchants and bankers lent money to princes across the region, and the city controlled a sizable rural territory. Metz fought the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Lorraine</a> and withstood a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vii-of-france">Charles VII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a> in 1444.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1552 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-france">Henry II of France</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-chambord">Treaty of Chambord</a> with German Protestant princes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> besieged it in the winter of 1552–1553 but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-duke-of-guise">Francis of Guise</a> held it. Metz became French, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> confirmed in 1648.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1189 CE – 1552 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.36%;width:7.22%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1189 CE – 1552 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Metz</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Metz, Metz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1370&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-metz" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Metz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4823 464 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Metz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5143 720L5229 694M5143 720L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5143 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5143 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Metz. <a href="/atlas?year=1370&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-metz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

